ZTEXT00100004ca82be4bafc0000000
Zero - shared language and target contract

SYNTAX.ztext defines the data-first source grammar used by all current runtime
sources. compiler.zero is the unified compiler written in Zero. It emits
x86-64 Linux ELF, raw64 and PE32+ EFI code, raw32 and PE32 IA32 EFI code
while preserving full u64 values, a restricted real-mode u32 subset for
bios.zero, native Windows x64 console PE files, and ZEROAPP1 guest applications
for Zero OS on x64/IA32. It emits native code directly without an assembler
or linker. Ezero is a separate high-level language implemented on this Zero
foundation; its inferred values/phrases/functions do not change Zero grammar.
See X86.ztext and REAL.ztext for the differing target/entry contracts.

Source and statements
Identifiers are case-sensitive ASCII names, at most 31 bytes; references and
binding targets use @name. Comments start with # and continue to newline.
Whitespace is space/tab/CR/LF. Integers use decimal or 0x hexadecimal syntax.
Strings are NUL-terminated ASCII, at most 4095 data bytes, with escapes
\n \r \t \\ \". String values are pointers to read-only embedded bytes.

    10 => @name.
    @name + 1 -> @name.
    ? @name == 11 [ !put[65]. ] ~ [ !put[66]. ]
    ?? @name < 20 [ @name + 1 -> @name. ]

=> declares a unique top-level variable after evaluating its initializer.
-> assigns an existing variable. Declarations inside blocks are rejected.
Names persist to program end and cannot shadow another name. All operation
names are reserved across targets, even where the operation is unavailable;
retired keywords are also reserved. ? is a conditional
and ?? is a loop; the optional ~ block is the conditional's alternative.
Control blocks use square brackets and do not take a trailing period.

Expressions
From high to low precedence: grouping/literals/@references/:operations,
unary -, multiplication *, left-associative +/-, and one unsigned comparison
(== != < <= > >=). Zero is false; any other value is true. Comparisons return
0 or 1. The x86-64 and protected-mode IA32 compilers use u64 values with
modulo 2^64 arithmetic; the real-mode subset uses u32 and modulo 2^32.
There is no signed coercion or division. Literal overflow for a target is
rejected. There are no user-defined functions, named types, imports or inline
assembly. Parentheses group arithmetic; operation arguments use [ ] and |.

:bitxor[@left | @right] and :bitand[@left | @right] operate on all value bits.
:shr[@value | @count] performs an unsigned logical right shift. Counts>=64
return 0 on x64/IA32, and counts>=32 return 0 in real mode. IA32 preserves full
u64 count semantics, so any nonzero high count word returns 0. These use the
normal operation brackets; no new infix punctuation or precedence is added.

General value operations
:load8[@address], :load16[@address], :load32[@address], :load64[@address]
read the indicated width; smaller loads zero-extend to the target value width.
:reserve[@size] allocates from the target's fixed arena with 16-byte alignment.
Allocations are not automatically zero-filled or freed. Overflow/exhaustion
traps with UD2. There are no general pointer bounds checks or exception
recovery; source must supply valid owned/mapped addresses.
The raw/UEFI x86-64/IA32 arena is 64 KiB. Real mode has an 8 KiB low-memory
arena and does not support load64/store64. Hosted arena size is documented
below; it is independent of the kernel and firmware arena contracts.

Linux x86-64 ELF targets
build/zero < source > executable
build/zero --host < source > executable

Both emit native ELF64 directly with a 4 MiB allocation arena. Earlier hosted
versions used 64 KiB and 1 MiB; raw/UEFI arena sizes are unchanged.
Default mode disallows memory stores;
--host permits stores in owned process memory for image-building tools.
!put[@value]. writes one low byte, retries EINTR and exits 74 on other errors.
!exit[@value]. exits using the low 8 bits of its argument.
:read[@address | @count] and :write[@address | @count] perform one Linux
stdin/stdout syscall, returning a byte count or negative errno encoded in u64.
Callers handle partial I/O, EOF and errors. read writes into the supplied
buffer. Falling off hosted source exits0. Host SIGPIPE behavior applies.
:pread[@fd | @buffer | @count | @offset] and :pwrite with the same arguments
perform one positioned Linux I/O call using a full 64-bit offset. They do not
change the descriptor's current position. :fsync[@fd] flushes the descriptor.
Each returns the direct u64 representation of the syscall result, including
negative errno; the caller handles EINTR, short transfers and failures.
Descriptors are supplied by the host invocation; no file-opening API is added.
A generated executable may need chmod +x. The IA32 compiler has no hosted
ELF target and does not accept these Linux operations.

Windows x64 console target
build/zero --windows < source > program.exe

This emits an original native PE64 console application with a 4 MiB Zero arena
and a committed 16 MiB Windows stack. It directly imports KERNEL32, with no CRT,
Linux dependency, assembler or foreign-language runtime. read/write use binary
standard handles; counts above 0x7fffffff return unsigned -22, EOF returns 0,
other I/O errors return unsigned -5, and short transfers remain visible.
put exits 74 on a failed single-byte write; exit uses its low 8 status bits.
Memory operations, bit operations and cooperative fork/switch are supported;
ports, BIOS/UEFI operations and Unix pread/pwrite/fsync are rejected.
Native startup converts GetCommandLineW to UTF-8 and parses at most 4095 bytes
and 63 user arguments (excluding the executable). Its own parser handles
spaces/tabs, quote grouping, empty arguments and backslashes preceding quotes.
Malformed quoting or an exceeded bound exits 64. See
portability/host/windows-README.ztext for precise host behavior and test scope.
Tests use Wine; native Microsoft Windows execution remains unverified.

Zero OS application targets
build/zero --app64 < source > program.zeroapp
build/zero --app32 < source > program.zeroapp

Both produce native position-independent ZEROAPP1 images with a 64-byte header,
full u64 language values and a 4 MiB owned application arena. They use bounded
memory input/output streams supplied by Zero OS instead of Linux/Windows or
firmware services. read returns available input bytes and then 0 at EOF; write
returns a short count or unsigned -28 when full; put exits 74 when full.
exit or fallthrough returns to the caller. Memory, bit operations and arg0/arg1
are available; ports, firmware, foreign calls, fork/switch and positioned file
I/O are rejected. The caller must validate the image, architecture, context,
stack region and buffer ownership. There is no process isolation or preemption;
unsafe Zero stores or non-yielding programs can affect the shared kernel.
The precise 192-byte context, ZEROAPP1 header and entry/return contracts are in
portability/guest/ABI.ztext. Host ELF/PE executables are not guest app images.

Native host services (Linux ELF and Windows PE)
All operations below use ':' and return a value, including file mutations.
:host[] returns 1 for Linux, 2 for Windows and 0 on every guest/firmware target.
:argcount[] counts user arguments, excluding the executable name.
:argument[index] uses one-based user indices and returns a persistent pointer
to a NUL-terminated argument, or 0 for an invalid index. Windows converts wide
arguments to UTF-8; Linux preserves the supplied bytes. The original process
frame is retained across cooperative fork/switch, so child argument access
remains valid. :program[] returns the absolute executable path or 0, rather
than trusting argv0. Cache it: Linux consumes 4096 arena bytes per successful
call; Windows supplies a stable startup buffer.

:fileopen[path | mode] returns an opaque handle or unsigned -1:
  0 read existing
  1 exclusive create for writing
  2 create or truncate for writing
  3 read/write existing
  4 exclusive create executable output for writing
Linux new-file modes 1/2 use 0600 and mode 4 uses 0700, reduced by umask; truncating
an existing file preserves its permissions. Windows uses normal file ACLs.
Paths are nonzero NUL-terminated strings of at most 4095 bytes. Linux passes
filesystem bytes directly; Windows validates UTF-8 and uses wide file APIs.
Opening requires a regular file and rejects a final symlink/reparse point.
Type validation precedes mode 2 truncation; Linux FIFO probes do not block.
Ancestor links still follow normal OS resolution; this is not a sandbox.

:fileread[handle | buffer | count] returns bytes read, 0 at EOF, or unsigned -1.
:filewrite[handle | buffer | count] returns bytes written or unsigned -1.
Short transfers remain visible; callers loop to complete a whole file.
Linux retries interrupted file reads/writes. Windows count is at most 0x7fffffff.
:filesize[handle] returns bytes without changing the offset, or unsigned -1.
:fileclose[handle] returns 0 or unsigned -1; Linux does not retry failed close.
:filesync[handle] requests synchronization of the opened file using Linux
fsync (with EINTR retry) or Windows FlushFileBuffers, returning0 or unsigned-1.
It neither closes the handle nor changes its offset. Portable callers use an
owned writable regular-file handle. This requests a file flush, not directory
or rename durability, an atomic transaction, or proof of power-loss safety.
:filedelete[path] removes a file; :makedir[path] creates one directory;
:dirempty[path] removes only an empty directory. :filemove[source | destination]
atomically renames on the same filesystem without replacing an existing
entry or falling back to copy/delete. These mutations return 0 or unsigned -1.
Errors are normalized: -1 alone cannot distinguish missing from inaccessible.

:hostwait[milliseconds] suspends the current native thread for an interval in
0..86400000 inclusive, returning0. Larger unsigned values immediately return
unsigned-1. Linux retries interrupted nanosleep with the remaining timespec;
other native failures return unsigned-1. Windows uses Sleep. Scheduling may
lengthen the delay. Zero is allowed and may yield to the native scheduler.
This is not a wall clock, a deadline guarantee, a background service, or a
switch between Zero cooperative tasks.

:hostalloc[bytes] obtains an OS allocation with read/write protection, returning
a page-aligned pointer or 0. :hostprotect[address | bytes | execute] selects
read/execute when execute=1 or read/write when execute=0, returning0 or
unsigned -1; other execute values fail. It never requests writable executable
pages. :hostfree[address | bytes] releases an owned allocation, returning0 or
unsigned -1. Caller ownership, valid ranges and lifetimes are explicit; these
operations add no pointer tracking or process isolation. Windows frees the
complete original allocation; Linux uses the supplied unmap length.
The existing :call uses the Microsoft x64 ABI in --host/--windows, suitable
for Zero's own application entry contract. It is not an implicit bridge to
Linux functions using a different calling convention.

--app64/--app32 accept these 18 operations for a shared Ezero runtime. host,
argcount, argument, program and hostalloc return 0; file operations,
hostprotect, hostfree and hostwait return unsigned -1 after evaluating operands.
They perform no host filesystem access or sleeping. Raw/EFI/real accept only host[] from
this extension and reject its other operations. Native call remains rejected
by guest applications. See portability/host/ABI.ztext for IDs and emission ABI.

Memory and port effects
!store8[@address | @value]. writes the low 8 bits. !store16, !store32 and
!store64 use the same argument form and their corresponding widths.
:in8[@port] / :in16[@port] / :in32[@port] read zero-extended port values.
!out8[@port | @value]. / !out16[@port | @value]. / !out32[@port | @value].
use the low 16 port bits and the low 8/16/32 value bits respectively. All widths
work on raw/UEFI x64/IA32 and real mode. Linux, Windows and ZEROAPP1
application targets reject hardware port operations.
See selfhost/port32.ztext for the dword extension and native PCI probe evidence.

Raw and UEFI x86-64 targets
build/zero --raw < source > kernel.bin
build/zero --efi < source > BOOTX64.EFI

:arg0[] and :arg1[] return initial RCX/RDX. UEFI supplies the image handle
and system-table pointer; the raw kernel receives our handoff in arg0.
!disable. disables maskable interrupts. !halt. disables interrupts and loops
in HLT. :call[@fn | @a1 | @a2 | @a3 | @a4 | @a5] evaluates left-to-right and
uses the Microsoft x64 ABI, with stack alignment, shadow space and a fifth
stack argument. The callee must follow that ABI; the result is RAX.
:call7[@fn | @a1 | @a2 | @a3 | @a4 | @a5 | @a6 | @a7] extends this
firmware call to seven arguments on --raw and --efi. The first four use
RCX/RDX/R8/R9; the last three use stack slots after 32-byte shadow space.
Both calls align the stack and preserve the caller operand stack and Zero
arena state, including when calls are nested inside expressions. The callee
must preserve Microsoft x64 nonvolatile registers. call7 remains available
on IA32 raw/EFI with its existing IA32 ABI; other targets reject call7.
!enter[@entry | @arg | @stack_top]. disables interrupts, installs an aligned
stack, puts arg in RCX and jumps to entry. It never returns.
Falling off raw/EFI source halts. Hosted put/exit/read/write/pread/pwrite/fsync
are rejected.
Ports, arg0/arg1, enter, disable and halt are unavailable in Linux targets.
The explicit Microsoft-ABI call operation is also supported by --host and
--windows for owned native code; default ELF continues to reject call.

Real-mode BIOS block transfer
:biosmove[descriptor_table | word_count] invokes BIOS INT15/AH87 only on the
--real target. The caller owns a 48-byte descriptor table at a physical address
<=0xffd0; word_count is 1..32768. Invalid arguments return 0xffffffff before a
firmware call. Success returns 0; CF failure returns BIOS AH or 256 if AH is 0.
The shim preserves general/segment registers and EFLAGS except the EAX result,
then clears DF. The caller supplies all descriptors and usable-memory policy,
handles firmware failure, and reenables A20 before direct high-memory access.
See portability/guest/biosmove/README.ztext for exact table fields and verified
4/8/32MiB copies. Other BIOS implementations can have 24-bit limitations; this
is not a universal 4 GiB transfer guarantee. Every non-real target rejects it.

Protected-mode IA32 targets
build/zero --raw32 < source > kernel32.bin
build/zero --efi32 < source > BOOTIA32.EFI

Values remain full u64, represented by paired 32-bit registers. Pointer values
must fit 32 bits. Entry args and firmware calls use an explicit IA32 cdecl ABI.
:call supplies five 32-bit stack words; the additional :call7 operation supplies
seven words, including split by-value firmware UINT64 parameters when needed.
Firmware return EAX is zero-extended, so EFI_STATUS errors have bit 31 set.
These are explicit ABI operations, not an implicit reduction of language values.
See X86.ztext and UEFI32.ztext before using them. No PAE or long mode is required
by generated IA32 kernel code.

Cooperative native continuations
:fork[@context | @stack_base | @stack_bytes] creates a suspended child.
The parent receives 0. When !switch[@from_context | @to_context]. first
activates the child, the same fork expression receives 1 in that child.
switch saves the current continuation and restores an initialized one.
These operations exist in Linux/Windows hosted x64, raw/UEFI x64 and
raw/UEFI IA32 targets; real mode and ZEROAPP1 application targets reject them.
All arguments evaluate left-to-right.

A context is 80 bytes aligned to 8, with ten u64 fields: program counter,
native SP, native BP, arena cursor, arg0, arg1, flags, resume result, arena
base and version. IA32 clears each high half. Version 1 is published only
after initialization. Callers must provide owned valid memory; a context
must not overlap its child stack, and switch needs distinct context records.
The child stack base is 16-byte aligned and its size at least 81920 bytes.
Runtime guards check address arithmetic, live-stack room and relevant
overlaps; detected violations trap with UD2. These are not memory protection.

fork copies the 512-byte variable frame and live expression operands onto
the child's independent stack. The child has a new private 64 KiB allocation
arena. Existing pointer values remain unchanged and reference shared memory;
they are not guessed or relocated. This also applies to a hosted parent with
a 4 MiB arena. The caller controls when to switch and how to schedule workers.
There are no implicit timer interrupts, preemption, address-space isolation
or SMP synchronization. See selfhost/CORE.ztext for the exact native ABI.

Entry, storage and limits
Raw64 requires long mode/CPL0 and valid mappings. Raw32 requires protected
mode/CPL0 and flat accessible code/data segments; paging may be disabled.
Both require at least 80 KiB below the entry stack pointer for the 64 KiB arena,
64 eight-byte variables, saved entry state and temporary stack. Our loaders
provide a stack pool containing the main stack and two child-stack regions:
UEFI allocates 1 MiB. BIOS validates its high-memory pool using E820 records;
the selected addresses and boot-image slots are loader policy in BIOS.ztext.
Raw/firmware top-level programs never return. Hosted exit and ZEROAPP1
return behavior follow their separate target contracts.

Source limit 524288 bytes; token limit 240000; output limit 524288 bytes,
including executable metadata; 64 variables; 64 nested blocks; 64 expression levels including the
leaf. Thus 63 nested parentheses plus the leaf reach the expression limit.
EFI headers/padding/relocations count toward output size. Real-mode output
has a separate 16 KiB bound described in REAL.ztext.

Errors and implementation boundary
Compile errors return 1 and emit no stdout. This compiler version does not
yet print a source diagnostic; callers must check its exit status.
All source validation finishes before output writing begins. An I/O failure
can leave partial output.
Native compiler launchers are themselves Zero and load the original Zero/Ezero
application images through the host memory services; no shell, Python or
foreign-language runtime is required by those launchers. The complete boot ISO
is built in process by the original tools/build.zero.
The compiler source is entirely Zero and can be built for Linux x86-64,
Windows x64 or the ZEROAPP1 guest ABI. The Linux Zero-built seed rebuilds
compiler.zero, with a second generation compared byte for byte. The same
source and grammar drive all output targets; target choices are explicit.
Neither the compiler nor generated Zero code passes through an assembler
or linker in the current build. Compiler
templates encode language primitives, instructions, entry/firmware ABIs and
file metadata. Loader, table, device and filesystem policy stays in Zero.

Native GUI interfaces (0.13)
hostsyscall[number|a1|a2|a3|a4|a5|a6] performs an x86-64 Linux system call;
hostlibrary[name], hostsymbol[library|name] and
hostcall12[address|a1|a2|a3|a4|a5|a6|a7|a8|a9|a10|a11|a12] expose documented
Windows x64 library interfaces. Arguments and results are raw integer/pointer
values. These are unsafe native interoperability primitives, not a sandbox.
Opposite-host operations return failure values. Guest and firmware targets
reject these host-only primitives. The original GUI editor uses them without
importing a GUI toolkit, foreign compiler or foreign source.
